<com:TContent ID="body" >
<h1>Installing PRADO</h1>
<div id="install-info" class="block-content">
<p>
If you are viewing this page from your own Web server, you are already done with the installation.
</p>
</div>

<h2>Requirements</h2>

<p class="block-content">
PRADO 4 needs PHP 8.1 or later. It runs on any web server that serves PHP, and it has been
tested with Apache on Linux, macOS and Windows.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
Six PHP extensions are required. Composer refuses to install without them.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Extension</th><th>Used for</th></tr>
<tr><td><tt>ctype</tt>, <tt>pcre</tt>, <tt>spl</tt></td><td>Core framework operation.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>dom</tt></td><td>Reading XML configurations and templates.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>intl</tt></td><td>Cultures, dates, numbers and message formatting.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>json</tt></td><td>Callback requests and JSON responses.</td></tr>
</table>

<p class="block-content">
Several more are optional, each one enabling a particular part of the framework.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Extension</th><th>Enables</th></tr>
<tr><td><tt>pdo</tt></td><td>Every database component, including <a href="?page=Database.ActiveRecord">Active Record</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>openssl</tt></td><td>Encryption of sensitive data such as the page state.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>mbstring</tt>, <tt>iconv</tt></td><td>Locale-aware string handling in the I18N components.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>apcu</tt>, <tt>memcached</tt></td><td>The matching cache modules.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>soap</tt></td><td><a href="?page=Services.SoapService">The SOAP service</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>xsl</tt></td><td><a href="?page=Controls.XmlTransform">TXmlTransform</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>zlib</tt></td><td>Output compression.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>sockets</tt></td><td>Capturing the log of a forked child process.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>hash</tt></td><td>Hash algorithms beyond md5 and sha1.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>xdebug</tt></td><td>Stack traces on fatal errors, during development.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Starting a new application</h2>

<p class="block-content">
PRADO is installed with <a href="https://getcomposer.org/">Composer</a>. If you do not have
Composer yet, install it first, following the instructions on its own site. Then create the
application:
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="cli" CssClass="source block-content">
composer create-project pradosoft/prado-app app
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
The application is created in the <tt>app</tt> directory, with the entry script, the
configuration and an empty page tree already in place. Point a web server at that directory
and the application answers.
</p>

<h2>Adding PRADO to an existing application</h2>

<p class="block-content">
Add the framework as a dependency instead. PRADO's client-side libraries are distributed as
Bower packages, so the <a href="https://asset-packagist.org">asset-packagist</a> repository
has to be declared alongside it.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="json" CssClass="source block-content">
{
  "repositories": [
    {
      "type": "composer",
      "url": "https://asset-packagist.org"
    }
  ],
  "require": {
    "pradosoft/prado": "^4"
  }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="cli" CssClass="source block-content">
composer install
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
Then include the autoloader, and the framework classes are available:
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;?php
require_once('vendor/autoload.php');
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>Directory permissions</h2>

<p class="block-content">
Two directories must be writable by the user the web server runs as. Composer sets the
permissions during installation, and they are worth checking after a deployment that
copies files.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Directory</th><th>Holds</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>assets</tt></td>
	<td>Images, stylesheets and scripts that the framework publishes from inside the application. See <a href="?page=Advanced.Assets">assets</a>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>protected/runtime</tt></td>
	<td>Compiled templates, cached data, application state and logs.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h2>Checking the installation</h2>

<p class="block-content">
The command line tool reports the version it is running, which confirms that the
autoloader and the framework are in place.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="cli" CssClass="source block-content">
./vendor/bin/prado-cli
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
It can also serve the application without a web server, which is the quickest way to see a
new application answer. See <a href="?page=GettingStarted.CommandLine">the command line
tool</a>.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="cli" CssClass="source block-content">
./vendor/bin/prado-cli http
</com:TTextHighlighter>

</com:TContent>
